{
  "question": "Trace how our understanding of water on Mars changed over time.",
  "plan": {
    "sub_questions": [
      "What early observations and hypotheses (telescopes, Mariner/Viking era) shaped initial ideas about water on Mars?",
      "What geomorphic and mineralogical evidence indicates Mars once had abundant liquid water?",
      "What discoveries show present-day water or water ice (polar caps, subsurface ice, Phoenix, orbiters, rover observations)?",
      "How did successive missions change the implications for past habitability and climate evolution?"
    ],
    "phrasings": [
      "Trace how our understanding of water on Mars changed over time.",
      "Early telescopic observations and Mariner images identified polar caps and surface features suggestive of water",
      "Viking landers analyzed Martian soil and returned inconclusive results on organics and aqueous chemistry",
      "Orbital imagery revealed valley networks and large outflow channels carved by flowing water in Mars' past",
      "The twin rovers discovered sedimentary rocks and minerals formed in ancient aqueous environments",
      "Phoenix confirmed subsurface water ice at high latitudes through trenching and in-situ observations",
      "Modern orbiters and rovers detected hydrated minerals and seasonal changes consistent with present-day ice and transient brines"
    ],
    "visual_phrasings": [
      "Orbital view of branching valley networks and massive outflow channels cutting the Martian surface",
      "Rover images of layered sedimentary outcrops with cross-bedding and mineral veins indicating past water",
      "Close-up of a lander or rover scoop revealing bright subsurface ice or frost in the trench"
    ],
    "needs_chronology": true,
    "rationale": "I broke the question into temporal and evidence-based aspects\u2014early ideas, geological/mineral evidence for ancient water, observations of present-day ice, and how missions shifted interpretations of habitability\u2014so searches can trace changes over time."
  },
  "answer": {
    "answer": "Early flyby imagery from Mariner 4 in 1965 presented a heavily cratered, apparently dry Mars and set an early view of the planet as largely without surface water [1]. Beginning with the 2004\u20132005 rover era, Spirit and Opportunity and related mission footage and scenes document in-situ investigations (drilling, rock studies) that moved interpretations toward past water activity on Mars [4][5][7][8]. By the 2010s and into the 2020s, orbiter and rover results were described in archival pieces as supporting specific hypotheses of ancient oceans, river channels, and aqueous alteration, with items in 2015, 2021 and 2022 highlighting oceans, river channels, and new clues to a watery past [11][13][14]. Altogether the archive traces a clear shift from an initial impression of a dry, cratered world to a consensus that liquid water played a major role in Mars\u2019 history, refined over decades of missions [1][11][14].",
    "citations": [
      1,
      4,
      5,
      7,
      8,
      11,
      13,
      14
    ],
